    "body": "### Problem\n\nAs discussed in [this comment](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/40846#issuecomment-5207142158), the Lua-based zip plugin introduced in #40846 is read-only, which is its limitation compared to the legacy plugin it replaced as the default, so changes cannot be written back into the archive.\n\n Just for record, The legacy Vimscript plugin (now old-zip, activated via :packadd old-zip) supports:\n\n - Updating an existing archive member by writing to its zipfile:// buffer,\n - Extracting the selected entry to the filesystem with the x command,\n - Custom backends via the g:zip_* variables and a PowerShell fallback.\n\n Per the new :help zip-limitations, if someone wants this feature, the documented workaround today is to fall back to old-zip:\n```\nThe legacy Vimscript zip plugin browses, updates, and extracts archive\nentries. It is not loaded by default; use `:packadd` to activate it: >lua\n\n vim.cmd.packadd('old-zip')\n```\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n:edit archive.zip\n:edit zipfile:///path/to/archive.zip::src/main.c\n(type some edit command like `i`)\nE21: Cannot make changes, 'modifiable' is off\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nWork like the old-zip, could edit text file, and write it, then update the zip ball.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\n0.13 commit 6cdab15\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nno\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nNixOS unstable\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nghostty\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-ghostty\n\n### Installation\n\nUsing https://github.com/nix-community/neovim-nightly-overlay",
